\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Ambiguity in NLP and requirements engineering.}
\citet{li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} propose an 11-type NLP taxonomy covering syntactic, semantic, and pragmatic ambiguity, from which we draw our linguistic-layer types; \citet{kamath_scope_2024} find systematic surface-scope preferences in LLMs. In requirements engineering, \citet{massey_ambiguity_re_2014} classify ambiguity types in regulatory texts, and \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish nocuous ambiguity (stakeholders diverge) from innocuous (they converge). Computational detection has progressed from heuristic \citep{chantree_2006} through NLP-based \citep{ferrari_ambiguity_2019} to transformer-based methods \citep{ezzini_taphsir_2022}. We extend Berry and Kamsties' framework to LLM agents, where the absence of shared organizational context makes previously innocuous ambiguity nocuous.

\paragraph{Psycholinguistics of ambiguity processing.}
\citet{ferreira_good_enough_2002} show that humans build ``good-enough'' representations rather than full parses, paralleling our observation that LLM agents adopt surface interpretations; \citet{li_garden_path_llm_2024} demonstrate analogous garden-path effects in LLMs. \citet{liu_ambiguity_2023} show that language models collapse multiple interpretations into a single output; \citet{gur_arieh_ambiguity_collapse_2026} taxonomize the epistemic risks of this collapse. These findings ground our \emph{disambiguation neglect} result: agents process ambiguous input without meta-cognitive awareness but, unlike humans, lack social channels to recover.

\paragraph{Agent safety and policy specification.}
Agent safety benchmarks evaluate risk awareness \citep{rjudge_2024}, tool-use safety \citep{toolemu_2024}, and policy compliance \citep{yin_safeagentbench_2025,phantompolicy_2026}; our work differs by focusing on the \emph{source specification} rather than agent execution. DRIP-R \citep{drip_r_2026} is the closest concurrent work, benchmarking 400 policy-ambiguous retail scenarios across five RE ambiguity types. Our matched-pair design establishes causality (vs.\ DRIP-R's correlational design), measures safety violations (vs.\ decision quality), and adds linguistic-layer types (Appendix~\ref{app:drip_mapping}); our perturbation controls ($\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$\,pp) isolate the causal contribution. SOPBench \citep{li_sopbench_2025} benchmarks SOP compliance with naturally occurring ambiguity; we use it for cross-benchmark evaluation (\S\ref{sec:results}). $\tau$-bench \citep{tau_bench_2024} and $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025} provide our evaluation platform. PolicyBank \citep{policybank_2026} studies iterative policy refinement; \citet{krakovna2020specification} document specification gaming in RL agents, a reward-specification analogue of our findings.

We evaluate on 500 policy clauses (250 ambiguous, 250 unambiguous) using stratified 5-fold cross-validation, with clause pairs kept in the same fold to prevent leakage. Table~\ref{tab:detection} reports per-type results.

\begin{table}[!ht]
\centering
\small
\caption{Proof-of-concept detection classifier (GPT-4.1, zero-shot, 5-fold CV). Binary ambiguity detection: F1\,=\,0.81, FPR\,=\,4.4\%. Six-class macro F1\,=\,0.63.}
\label{tab:detection}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
Type & Prec. & Recall & F1 \\
\midrule
Cond.\ precedence & 0.96 & 0.92 & 0.94 \\
Scopal & 0.91 & 0.64 & 0.75 \\
Auth.\ scope & 1.00 & 0.36 & 0.53 \\
Lexical & 0.36 & 0.42 & 0.39 \\
Incompleteness & 0.48 & 0.26 & 0.34 \\
\midrule
No ambiguity & 0.76 & 0.96 & 0.85 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Detection difficulty is inversely correlated with agent vulnerability: incompleteness, the most dangerous type (53.6\% violation rate, \S\ref{sec:results}), is the hardest to detect (F1\,=\,0.34), while conditional precedence (36.8\% violation rate) is the easiest (F1\,=\,0.94). This asymmetry arises because types with overt linguistic signals (e.g., conditional structures) are readily detectable but only moderately dangerous, whereas missing information leaves no surface trace for a classifier to exploit.

The primary confusion pathway is authorization scope $\to$ lexical (48\% of authorization scope clauses): authorization boundary clauses frequently contain polysemous terms, causing the classifier to attribute the ambiguity to word-level rather than permission-level underspecification.

These results position automated detection as a viable first-pass screening tool (binary F1\,=\,0.81) but an unreliable type classifier, particularly for the highest-risk types. The classifier was evaluated on synthetic clauses derived from $\tau^2$-bench; generalization to naturally occurring policy documents requires further validation.

\section{Replication on Additional Model Families}
\label{app:model_expansion}

To test cross-architecture generalization beyond the five main-study models, we replicate the core analyses on two additional model families: Gemini-2.5-Flash (Google) and LLaMA-4-Scout (Meta), each with 250 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $= 500$ episodes judged by GPT-5.4. These models represent two additional providers not in the original study. We exclude coreferential ambiguity because its disambiguation relies on benchmark-specific assertion structure rather than clause-level text changes (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}), limiting cross-benchmark generalizability. Tables~\ref{tab:expansion_overall} and~\ref{tab:expansion_pertype} report the results.

